  "0 - 500m": "Read at all hundred and twenty-eight settings on 01 50, and at seven of them on 01 51, which agreed at every one. The step changes five times: a tenth of a millisecond to fifty, then a half, then one, then ten, then twenty to setting a hundred and twenty-six, where the printed end is reached and the last setting repeats it.",
  "0 - 100m": "Read at all hundred and twenty-eight settings on 01 40. It shares the longer range's first two steps exactly -- a tenth of a millisecond to setting fifty, then a half to sixty -- and its third step is the same size and runs further: one millisecond to setting a hundred rather than to ninety. Then two milliseconds to setting a hundred and twenty-five, where the printed end is reached and the last two settings repeat it.",
  "200m - 990m/1sec": "Read at all hundred and twenty-eight settings on 01 52. The only range in this class whose page prints a time at the bottom as well as the top, and the byte starts there: setting zero is two hundred milliseconds, not nothing. Five milliseconds a setting to setting seventy, then ten to setting a hundred and fifteen, where the printed end is reached at one second and the last twelve settings repeat it. Every entry on this range is already a whole step of the clock, so nothing here tells the cut from the uncut ladder and this range is no evidence about the clock.",
  "0m - 635m": "Read at all hundred and twenty-eight settings on 04 00. The only range in this class read so far whose step never changes: five milliseconds a setting from the first setting to the last, reaching the printed end at the last setting rather than before it. Setting zero is no delay at all. Five milliseconds is a hundred and sixty whole steps of the clock, so every entry is already on it and this range is no evidence about the cut."
